The WASM + Elixir Kernel — the single go-forward plan  #plan #wb-sjg3

The complete build, moat included. This folds the wasmer migration (done), the blue-sky→reality verdict
([[moat-reality-verdict]]), the density measurement, and the AOT/two-tier insight into ONE plan we can
stand on. Discipline throughout: **every claim is a passing test or a published number. No cheap claims,
no missing links, no multi-engine filler.** Companion vision: [[beam-wasm-kernel]]; runtime split:
[[wasix-shell-migration]].

## THE FINAL PICTURE (the thing we stand on)

> **One literate `.work` source → compiled to two wasm substrates → executed as supervised,
> capability-secure, tenant-isolated BEAM Cells over ONE Membrane bus → at measured, published density →
> composable into cross-engine Fabric graphs.**

- The **BEAM is the kernel** — the scheduler, supervisor, router, tenancy + capability owner that wasm
  fundamentally lacks.
- **wasmer (WASIX subprocess)** runs the POSIX agent shell (real bash/coreutils/python over `/work`).
  **wasmex (wasmtime NIF, in-process)** runs typed components + the compiler lane. They are interchangeable
  EXECUTION SUBSTRATE; neither is the product.
- The **Membrane is the one security seam** — a wasmer-shell host-cap and a wasmex-component host-import
  resolve through the SAME grant + tenant resolver. This, made provable, IS the moat.
- The honest density line is **"hundreds of capability-secure multi-tenant cells per GB"** + a per-invocation
  AOT warm pool measured in **invocations/sec/GB** — NOT "thousands/billions."

## DONE (foundation, shipped + proven in prod)

Wasmer migration complete: agent shell on wasmer over /work (prod-verified), pinning + **true-offline
self-contained bundles** (bash dep stripped; runs under an unreachable registry), the **Membrane** (one
host-cap bus + WASIX-socket bridge — host caps compose mid-pipe), the **Session** (long-lived stateful
shell), the **toolkit compile+run lane** (C→Wasmer via wasix-cc + the Membrane route-around), the **Cell**
v1 (single-lane supervised envelope), dual-runtime decided on dated merit, docs/dead-code cleaned. All
green, in prod.

## THE PLAN — phases in truth-per-effort order (each gated by a PROOF)

### A — Prove the foundation (measure before building)
- **A1. Density baseline on a real Fly box.** Spawn N live wasmer Sessions AND N wasmex component cells;
  record per-cell RSS, cells-at-RSS-ceiling, cells/GB for BOTH lanes. PROOF: published numbers (extend
  `nexus/bench/density.exs`). This saves or sinks the cost narrative before any feature code.
- **A2. Decompose the 67 MB.** Measure python's share; establish a python-free "light cell" baseline. PROOF:
  light-cell vs full-cell RSS, published.

### B — The Membrane moat, made PROVABLE (the headline deliverable)
- **B1. `Nexus.Grant`** — one resolver: `Grant.resolve(grant, tenant) → %{caps, tenant}`, one capability
  vocabulary. Replaces the ad-hoc shell checks (`~w(net web browse)`, `~w(exec commands work)`) AND the
  component path (`Nexus.Capabilities.grants/1`).
- **B2. Wire BOTH lanes to it** — `sandbox.ex imports_for/2` (wasmex NIF import map) and the Membrane
  allowlist + `bash.ex permit/work_granted` (wasmer socket) both call `Nexus.Grant`. One source of truth.
- **B3. The GRANT-DRIFT test (green).** One grant list → a component Cell + a shell Cell → an ungranted cap
  (e.g. `fetch`) denied by the SAME code path on both; a granted cap allowed on both. PROOF: this test is
  the moat made executable. Today it would FAIL (two security paths) — making it green IS the moat work.
- **B4. Unified tenant binding** across both lanes the same way (no tenant supplied by the guest, ever).

### C — The Cell, made REAL (dual-lane, restart, meter)
- **C1. Dual-lane.** `Cell.spawn(unit)` routes a `server`/component → wasmex and a shell/`sandbox` → wasmer
  behind ONE `Cell.call`/`Cell.stop`. PROOF: dual-lane test — caller code byte-identical across engines.
  Until this passes, "one Cell over two engines" is filler.
- **C2. Real restart policy.** Drop `{:stop, :normal}` on crash; declare `:transient`; emit `{:DOWN, …}` to
  dependents. PROOF: kill the inner subprocess mid-run → supervisor restarts → dependent notified.
- **C3. Normalized meter.** Fold wasmex `StoreLimits` + wasmer `timeout_ms` + memory + output bytes into one
  `Cell.meter(pid)`. PROOF: per-tenant fairness + a ceiling that trips, read from the one meter.

### D — Density, made REAL (the two-tier model)
- **D1. AOT precompile.** Bake precompiled modules (`wasmer compile` / warm cache) at image build → no
  cold-start JIT. PROOF: cold vs warm spawn-ms, published.
- **D2. The warm POOL.** A supervised pool of stateless, AOT-loaded, memory-shared per-invocation boxes; a
  one-shot command checks out a warm box, runs, returns it. PROOF: invocations/sec/GB, published.
- **D3. Light-cell default.** Python out of the default env (opt-in heavyweight). PROOF: re-measured cells/GB.
- **D4. Publish the honest scorecard** — cells/GB + invocations/sec/GB + cold/warm spawn-ms, next to a Linux
  container baseline. This replaces every dead "billion on 1GB" line with a number we can defend.

### E — Fabric, the novel compute model (built ONLY on real Cells)
- **E1. A graph that targets a wasm ENGINE step** (a Cell per step), not just agents (today `flow.ex` can't).
- **E2. Cross-engine + durable.** A wasmer shell stage → a wasmex component stage → …, with BEAM messaging +
  Store persistence + per-step supervision + checkpoint between stages.
- **E3. The showcase.** PROOF: a running graph spanning both engines that NEITHER engine can do alone, with a
  mid-graph crash that resumes from the last checkpoint.

### F — Research spikes (labeled research, NOT roadmap; each gated on a pass/fail test)
- **F1. Shared-module + memfd-CoW** (wasmtime lane) — the ONLY path to a real "thousands per GB."
- **F2. In-process wasmer NIF vs subprocess** — resolve with the security tradeoff (subprocess = a real
  OS-process wall; NIF = single-runtime feel). Decide with evidence, don't assume.
- **F3. Distribution** — Cells across BEAM nodes (one nexus scaling horizontally).

### G — Keep the standard clean (THE LINE)
Generic mechanism only in the runtime; our opinions stay in the cloud app / our workbooks. Dual-runtime
documented; `vendor/wasmex` stays by design until F1 proves otherwise.

## THE DISCIPLINE (non-negotiable)
1. **No claim without proof** — a passing test or a published number, in the same commit.
2. **No multi-engine filler** — "one X over two engines" ships ONLY with the byte-identical/same-code-path
   test that proves it.
3. **Measure before building** — Phase A gates the rest; if a number kills a claim, restate the claim.
4. **Honest names** — plumbing is plumbing; the moat is exactly one thing (the Membrane grant seam) until a
   spike earns another.
